{
 "translation": "Then what happened? Sundarlal, squatting, said, \"What happened then?\" Rasalu also sat up on his cot and, coughing the characteristic cough of tobacco users, said, \"Has Lajwanti Bhabhi really come?\" Lalchand, continuing his story, said, \"At Wagah, Pakistan gave sixteen women, and in exchange, took sixteen women. But a dispute arose. Our volunteers were objecting that the women you gave are mostly middle-aged, old, and useless women. People gathered over this dispute. At that time, the volunteers from that side, showing Lajo Bhabhi, said, 'You call her old? Look, look, does even one of the women you gave compare to her?' And there, Lajo Bhabhi was hiding her tattoos from everyone's eyes. Then the dispute escalated. Both sides decided to take back their respective 'goods'. I shouted, 'Lajo, Lajo Bhabhi!' but the soldiers of our nation beat us and chased us away. And Lalchand began to show his elbow where he had been hit by a stick. Rasalu and Nekiram remained silent, and Sunderlal started looking far away. Perhaps he began to think, \"Lajo came, but didn't come.\" And from Sunderlal's face, it seemed as if he had crossed the desert of Bikaner and was now panting with his tongue out in the shade of some tree. He couldn't even manage to say, \"Give him water.\" He felt that the violence of before and after the Partition was still effective, only its form had changed. Now people no longer had the same hesitation as before. If you ask anyone, \"Did Lehna Singh live in Sambharwala, and his sister-in-law Banto?\" they would immediately say, \"They died.\" And after that, completely unaware of death and its meaning, completely indifferent, they would move on. Going one step further than this, with a very cold heart, human traders began to trade human goods, human flesh and skin, and exchange them. Cattle buyers used to open the jaw of a buffalo or cow and estimate its age from its teeth. Now, they started openly displaying the youth of a young woman, her glow, her most intimate secrets, her 'tandols'. Cruelty had now permeated the very being of these traders. Earlier, goods were sold in the market, and bargainers would shake hands, throw a handkerchief over them, and secretly make a deal as if the transaction was done with finger gestures under the handkerchief. Now, even the handkerchief for secret deals was gone, and transactions were happening openly, and people had forgotten the etiquette of trade. This whole transaction, this business, seemed like an old tale in which the story of the free buying and selling of women is narrated. Az-bayak stands before countless naked women, examining their bodies by touching them. And when he touches a woman's body with his finger, a pink mark appears on it, and around it, a pale circle, and then paleness and redness race to take each other's place. Az-bayak moves on, and the unacceptable woman, in a state of defeat and humiliation, holds her drawstring with one hand and hides her face from the public's gaze with the other, sobbing.",
